 a re-raise if the opponent is trapping with a set (e.g., 88, 77, 33). This move has a higher risk but the potential reward is high.\n\nMy opponent's pre-flop raise to 955 is a 3-bet, which is often a strong hand, which includes AA, KK, QQ, JJ, AK. The flop was K87 and they checked behind, which makes it unlikely that they've made AA. My range is KK or AK. My opponent checking back after their strong preflop range and the flop indicates weakness.\n\nConsidering these factors, I'm leaning toward checking. Checking on the Turn is standard in a 3-bet pot.\n\nI should check (0).\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation:\nThe situation is a 3-bet pot (pre-flop raise and re-raise) where the opponent checked back on the Flop. You hold Top Pair with the best Kicker (AK).\n1. **Check (0)**: This is the most prudent play. The opponent's check on the flop suggests they either have a medium pair (QQ, JJ) that is scared of the King, or they are trapping with a set/AA. If they have an underpair, betting will likely make them fold, so you gain no value. If you check, you may induce a bluff or get a call on the river.\n2. **Bet (e.g., 800-1200)**: Betting builds the pot but risks isolating yourself against better hands (sets, AA) or causing worse hands (QQ, JJ) to fold immediately.\n3. **All-in (-2)**: Overbetting the pot is a mistake here, as only hands that beat you will call.\n\nReason:\nYou have a strong hand (TPTK), but given the pre-flop aggression and the flop check, betting gives the opponent a chance to fold worse hands (denying you value) or raise with better hands. Checking allows you to keep their range wide, potentially inducing a bluff from a missed hand or getting a crying call on the river from an underpair.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
